speechtext:
They are as follows:
Leon Renault, L. N. Asselin, G. Bouliane, I'antaleon Courcy, Nazaire LeBel, Edmond Roy, Antoine Borne, Edmond Menard, Alphonse Pelletier, Joseph Dupont, Nazaire Grosleau, F. R. Roy, T. E. Flynn, Onesime Brochu. I read the names so that 90}
perhaps the hon. member will recognize some of his friends.

I would like to draw the attention of the minister to the first of these affidavits, in which the charge is made by John D. Eoss that Mr. Caldwell participated in the provincial election of 1908 and at a time when he was not employed in the department. The charge made by Mr. Eoss against Mr. Caldwell ante-dates the last election by five years. He says:
I know Mr. Arthur Caldwell, of New Carlisle, assistant inspector of weights and measures;
2nd. That at the provincial elections of 1908, Mr. Arthur Caldwell took an active part in the elections in favour of me Liberal candidate, J. H. Kelly;
3rd. That on nomination day, the said Arthur Caldwell placed the Masonic Hall at the disposition of the Liberal speakers without any authority from the officials of that body;
4th. I was present at the aforementioned

meeting and by the way Mr. Caldwell behaved on that day, I took him to be one of the Liberal leaders.
Does the minister think that is a very serious charge?

There are other affidavits. For instance, here is one by Daniel Walker, who says:
That I know Mr. Arthur Caldwell, assistant inspector of weights and measures, residing and domiciled in New Carlisle;
2.	That on the 17th September, 1912, to wit:
A few days before the election, I heard Mr. Caldwell canvassing at the door of the Presbyterian church in favour of the Liberal party.
There is another one, signed by J. E. Roy, in which he says:
That I know Mr. Arthur Caldwell, assistant inspector of weights and measures, said Mr. Arthur Caldwell living in New Carlisle;
2.	At the federal elections of 1908 I saw Mr. Caldwell standing at the door of the building where the poll was held, holding and checking a paper which he held in his hand and which to the best of my knowledge, I took for the electoral list;
3.	By the way Mr. Caldwell acted that day he gave me the impression that he was one of the Liberal leaders in New Carlisle.
Then there is the affidavit of Mr. Ross, which my hon. friend has read. James Edward Mill makes the following declaration :	*
1.	I know personally Arthur B. Caldwell, ot New Carlisle, in the said county and district, one of His Majesty's inspectors of weights and measures.
2.	The said inspector has oeen in the past, and is now ' an offensive partisan ' inconsistent with his duty as a federal employee;
3.	I know that the said inspector has always and is now taking an active part in local and federal politics in the said county and district;
4.	The said inspector has canvassed, and is still so doing as a Liberal;
5.	More especially, have I seen the said inspector as one of the principals, forming part of a political procession of Liberal views, during May, 1912, on or about, the tail end of the same being a Liberal dog deeKed in blue, and on different other occasions.
And I make this solemn declaration conscientiously believing it to be true.
